+### Experimental relative window input (Linux)
+
+`RvgWindowCallbacks.relative_pointer(ctx, dx, dy, buttons)` is appended at byte
+64, extending the table to 72 bytes. Tables of 56 or 64 bytes retain their prior
+behavior. Non-NULL opts the Linux window into SDL relative capture: motion is
+in host points (positive right/down), buttons use `RVG_WINDOW_BUTTON_*`, and
+button-only events have zero deltas. The capture click uses `pointer` first.
+The callback follows the same threading/lifetime rules as `pointer`. Other
+host platforms ignore it. If SDL refuses relative mode, input retains its
+absolute confinement and logs the refusal.
+
+By default a relative-input window display advertises no hardware cursor
+handlers on Linux, allowing the guest to render its cursor into the framebuffer.
+Hosts must verify their guest's software-cursor fallback before enabling it.
+
+The separate experimental `REIMS_VGPU_HOST_CURSOR_SOCKET` option retains cursor
+handlers and uses a local overlay with guest-layout feedback. In that mode the
+window routes captured input through the destination window's absolute `pointer`
+callback, with a 32768-unit coordinate range, instead of calling
+`relative_pointer` for each movement. The latter callback remains the capture
+opt-in. The companion protocol, limitations and acceptance status are documented
+in `scripts/multimonitor/README.md`.
