@@ -0,0 +1,191 @@
+# Guest-aware pointer companion (experimental)
+
+`guest_pointer.py` runs inside a macOS guest and maps each host window's
+normalized pointer coordinates into the matching guest display's current
+global desktop bounds. It needs Python 3 and existing Accessibility trust.
+It checks trust without requesting or changing permissions. It loads the
+macOS frameworks through `ctypes`; it needs no third-party Python package.
+
+This directory includes the guest endpoint, an SSH relay, and CPU tests.
+The paired QEMU input device is in `vendor/qemu/hw/input/reims-pointer.c`.
+No persistent launch, automatic startup, or deployment is provided here.
+
+## Identity and geometry
+
+The default map requires vendor 4102 and serial `head + 1`. That vendor was
+measured on the test guest. QEMU's `hw/display/apple-gfx.c` initializes
+`next_pgdisplay_serial_num` to 1 and increments it across all devices, so
+this default applies to the first/only apple-gfx device. `--vendor` and
+`--serial-base` support an explicit, independently verified alternate map.
+Display enumeration order and numeric CoreGraphics display IDs are never
+used to assign heads.
+
+The helper resolves every configured head before announcing readiness and
+again before each pointer packet. Missing, ambiguous, mirrored, or invalid
+maps fail closed. Bounds come from `CGDisplayBounds`, including negative
+origins and HiDPI logical dimensions; backing pixel dimensions are not used.
+The 32767 endpoint maps to the last coordinate inside a display, avoiding
+an accidental crossing onto a neighbor.
+
+## Transport protocol
+
+Run with the desired head count, for example:
+
+```sh
+/usr/bin/python3 guest_pointer.py --heads 2 --vendor 4102 --serial-base 1
+```
+
+A trusted local relay supplies ASCII lines on stdin:
+
+```text
+R 1
+P 1 HEAD X Y BUTTONS WHEEL_X WHEEL_Y
+```
+
+- `R 1` releases held buttons and clears click history at session start.
+- `HEAD` is zero-based and less than `--heads`.
+- `X` and `Y` are integers in 0..32767, relative to that head.
+- `BUTTONS` is the current mask: left=1, right=2, middle=4, back=8, forward=16.
+- Wheel values are signed integer notches: positive X is left; positive Y
+  is up. Each value must be in -32767..32767.
+
+Stdout contains only `READY 1\n`, emitted after native trust and initial
+map validation and recovery of any native held buttons from a previous helper. Diagnostics go to stderr. The relay must wait for readiness
+and close stdin on transport loss. EOF, reset, malformed packets, mapping
+failures, SIGTERM and SIGHUP release held buttons. Malformed input ends the session;
+it is not silently skipped. There is no idle timeout: without a heartbeat,
+an idle connection cannot be distinguished from an intentional held drag.
+A forced SIGKILL cannot run cleanup; the next helper startup queries native
+button state and releases only buttons still down, at the current cursor.
+
+Mouse transitions are explicit; dragging uses the previously held button
+state before release at the new coordinate. Left/right/middle/other-button
+events, mouse deltas, global session modifier flags, click counts, and both
+scroll axes are supplied to CoreGraphics. Double-click timing uses
+`NSEvent.doubleClickInterval`; proximity is limited to four desktop units
+on the same head. Moving a held drag outside that tolerance clears its click
+history. Applications may interpret side buttons differently.
+
+## Verification
+
+Run the CPU tests from the repository root:
+
+```sh
+python3 -B -m unittest discover -s scripts/multimonitor -p 'test_guest_pointer.py' -v
+```
+
+The 30 companion tests cover relative crossing and edge sliding, framing and ranges, display identity, negative origins,
+HiDPI geometry, live rearrangement, drag ordering, all button edges, click
+counts, wheels, resets, EOF, malformed input, hot unplug release failure, and native-state recovery after a helper crash.
+They use a fake backend and load no graphics framework or GPU.
+
+An Intel Tahoe 26.7 native smoke test in the integration investigation
+resolved all framework symbols and returned `READY 1` with existing
+Accessibility trust. A head-1 center packet independently read back at
+(2279.5, 719.5), the center of that guest's portrait display. That smoke test
+preceded the explicit mouse-delta fields; the integration's canvas tests
+must establish native click/drag/scroll and modifier behavior. Full
+host-window-to-guest acceptance and physical pointer use are separate gates.
+
+API references:
+[global display bounds](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/coregraphics/cgdisplaybounds(_:)),
+[click counts](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/coregraphics/cgeventfield/mouseeventclickstate),
+[scroll units](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/coregraphics/cgscrolleventunit).
+
+
+## Captured crossing (experimental Linux integration)
+
+Build this library and the paired QEMU changes together. Opt in with
+`REIMS_VGPU_RELATIVE_POINTER=1` and add a bridge connected to an explicit
+local socket:
+
+```text
+-chardev socket,id=pointer,path=/private/path/pointer.sock,server=on,wait=off
+-device reims-pointer,chardev=pointer,display=gfx,heads=2
+```
+
+Keep the socket in a private directory. The device accepts socket chardevs
+only, refuses migration, and drops input until the trusted companion is ready.
+Start the relay after the guest desktop and SSH are available:
+
+```sh
+python3 scripts/multimonitor/relay.py --socket /private/path/pointer.sock \
+  --ssh-host YOUR_GUEST --guest-helper /path/in/guest/guest_pointer.py --heads 2
+```
+
+A click establishes the guest position using the addressed display. While
+captured, SDL reports relative motion without a window-edge limit, retaining
+the host acceleration curve. `SDL_MOUSE_RELATIVE_SYSTEM_SCALE=0` explicitly
+requests raw motion instead. QEMU sends
+`M 1 HEAD DX DY BUTTONS WHEEL_X WHEEL_Y`; fields have the same limits as `P`,
+except deltas are signed -32767..32767 host points. `HEAD` identifies the source
+window, not the destination display. The companion follows the current guest
+desktop arrangement, preserving held buttons when crossing. Unmatched edges
+stop the cursor; diagonal motion slides along them. Gaps cannot be jumped.
+
+Arrange the displays inside macOS System Settings → Displays → Arrange, using
+an extended desktop. Linux window placement does not define guest positions.
+The companion reloads display bounds for every packet. Ctrl+Alt+Super releases
+capture and held buttons. Closing a display window retains QEMU's shutdown
+behavior. Keyboard focus stays in the capturing host window while the guest
+cursor crosses displays.
+
+Relative windows advertise no hardware cursor capability, requesting the
+guest's software-cursor fallback. An Intel Tahoe cold-boot check verified
+relative QEMU input crossing from the main display to its right-hand neighbor
+and back. Independent CoreGraphics position reads confirmed the crossing, and
+a framebuffer comparison showed the visible cursor on the destination display.
+Physical SDL border crossing was also confirmed, with a reported slow/heavy
+pointer feel. Captured motion now requests the host acceleration curve, but
+a physical retest still reported heavier movement than the original. The relative-only
+regression remains unresolved; the accepted local-overlay mode is described below. A stationary guest microbenchmark measured
+median relative-event processing of 0.56 ms, with one 32 ms outlier; it does
+not measure transport or visible cursor latency. Software-cursor frame latency
+and host-to-guest movement scaling still need investigation. Clicks after
+crossing and live rearrangement remain separate acceptance checks. Other host platforms keep
+their existing behavior.
+
+The relay handles fragmented readiness with a deadline, preserves packet order
+and queued releases on socket EOF, bounds queued input, and closes a stalled
+connection. SSH keepalives detect transport loss. `input_probe.swift` is an
+optional disposable AppKit test surface; its log records events actually
+received by each test window.
+
+```sh
+python3 -B -m unittest discover -s scripts/multimonitor -p 'test_*.py'
+python3 scripts/multimonitor/test_qemu_pointer.py --qemu /path/to/qemu-system-x86_64 \
+  --firmware-dir /path/to/qemu/share/qemu
+```
+
+
+## Experimental host cursor (local physical acceptance)
+
+Set `REIMS_VGPU_HOST_CURSOR_SOCKET` to an unused Unix socket path in a private
+local directory before launching the relative build. Pass the same path to the
+relay with `--layout-socket`. The helper's opt-in `--report-layout` mode emits
+`L 1 COUNT HEAD X Y WIDTH HEIGHT ...` after readiness and when display bounds
+change; it checks while idle every 250 ms. The relay forwards this bounded
+feedback stream to the window library. Disconnecting it releases capture.
+Rearranging displays also releases capture: click to capture again with the
+new layout. Ordinary single-monitor and relative-only modes are unchanged.
+
+The host integrates accelerated input locally using the guest display map and
+the destination window's scale. It sends absolute coordinates to the companion
+and draws its cursor at the same quantized location. A transparent SDL tooltip
+surface carries the guest cursor image independently of guest-frame rendering;
+it never takes focus or input. This is a host-rendered overlay, not the native
+hardware cursor plane, so equivalent latency is a measurement target, not an
+established result. Guest hardware-cursor callbacks remain enabled to suppress
+the software cursor in the guest framebuffer.
+
+The prototype is currently limited to one configured guest desktop per process.
+Linux enables SDL's renderer subsystem for the popup: Wayland's window-surface
+API uses a texture framebuffer and fails with video-only SDL builds.
+
+On Fedora/Wayland with the two-head Tahoe guest, the 2026-10-22 guarded physical
+test was accepted as "visible, aligned, and lighter" and "workable". The cursor
+surfaces created without errors after enabling SDL's renderer. This verifies the
+tested setup, not measured equivalence with hardware-cursor latency or all layouts.
+Source tests cover geometry and coordinate agreement. Broader drag, cursor-shape,
+scaling and rearrangement checks remain; guest-initiated cursor warps are not yet
+reconciled while captured. No normal launcher enables this mode by default.
